Unused auto-generated legacy ServiceAccount token Secrets are first marked invalid and later deleted. Auto-generated here means the Secret is referenced both ways by a ServiceAccount, through the ServiceAccount's secrets field.

The legacy ServiceAccount token cleaner does this. It runs in kube-controller-manager and checks every 24 hours. It first reads the ConfigMap created by legacy token tracking (kube-apiserver-legacy-service-account-token-tracking). If the current time is past that ConfigMap's date by the configured period, the cleaner checks every Secret of type kubernetes.io/service-account-token.

A Secret is marked invalid when all three of these are true:
- it is auto-generated;
- it is not mounted by any pod;
- it has not been used for the configured period since it was created or last used.

To mark it, the cleaner adds the label kubernetes.io/legacy-token-invalid-since with the current date. If the invalidated Secret stays unused for the period again, the cleaner deletes it.

The period defaults to one year. Administrators can change it with the kube-controller-manager flag --legacy-service-account-token-clean-up-period. The docs say this behavior applies from version 1.29.

If someone uses an invalidated token, the validator does four things:
- adds the audit annotation authentication.k8s.io/legacy-token-invalidated: <secret name>/<namespace>;
- increments the invalid_legacy_auto_token_uses_total metric;
- updates the kubernetes.io/legacy-token-last-used label;
- returns an error.

Users can then remove the invalid-since label to temporarily re-enable the token. The KEP also points them to the TokenRequest API instead.

If the tracking ConfigMap is unavailable, or the API server or etcd is down, no Secrets are removed. Workloads that still read auto-generated Secrets after they have been removed may fail.
